WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.109 --> 00:00:21.460
If a person breaks their ankle, and the bones
no longer line up with each other, a doctor

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:28.640
may need to perform a surgical procedure to
reposition and secure the bones.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.509
The ankle is the joint that connects the leg
and the foot.

00:00:32.509 --> 00:00:39.199
The ankle joint includes the two lower leg
bones called the tibia and fibula, and the

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:42.479
ankle bone called the tailus.

00:00:42.479 --> 00:00:49.640
Together, the end of the tibia and fibula
create a whartos or “slot” for the talus

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.660
which forms the bottom of the ankle joint.

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:59.640
Tissues called ligaments and tendons support
the ankle bones.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:06.899
Ligaments attach bones to bones, and tendons
attach muscles to bones.

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:13.149
The ankle joint allows the foot to move up
and down.

00:01:13.149 --> 00:01:21.040
Ankle injuries usually happen during athletic
activities, falls, or car accidents.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:26.080
The most common type of ankle fracture occurs
when the foot turns inward, and the ankle

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:28.820
rotates outward.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.900
If the fracture is stable, the pieces of bone
still line up in their normal position.

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:41.010
In any type of fracture, more than one bone
may break.

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:46.080
In a displaced fracture, the pieces of bone
no longer line up.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:53.700
If bone breaks through the skin, it’s known
as a compound or “open” fracture.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.820
In addition to broken bones, the ankle may
be sprained.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:02.560
This means the ligaments have been stretched
or torn.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:07.190
If the ankle is very swollen, the surgeon
may delay a surgical procedure to allow the

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:09.700
swelling to go down.

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.909
During this time, the surgeon may put the
ankle in a splint to provide support.

00:02:13.909 --> 00:02:19.959
During this time, the surgeon may recommend
elevating the ankle above the level of the

00:02:19.959 --> 00:02:23.409
heart and applying ice to it.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:30.510
To treat a stable ankle fracture, the healthcare
provider may put the leg in a cast or a boot

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:37.120
to keep the ankle from moving so that the
bones can heal together.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.760
Displaced and compound fractures with multiple
broken bones and torn ligaments may require

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.680
a surgical procedure.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.700
The most common surgical procedure to repair
a displaced open fracture is called an open

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.410
reduction with internal fixation.

00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:57.560
To begin, the surgeon will make an incision
over the area of the fracture.

00:02:57.560 --> 00:03:03.650
The surgeon will open up the area to feel
the pieces of bone and line them back up with

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.700
each other.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.660
A plate and screws will be used to keep them
aligned.

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:14.629
Additional screws may be inserted through
another incision to hold other fracture fragments

00:03:14.629 --> 00:03:16.959
in place.

00:03:16.959 --> 00:03:21.849
If the ligaments holding the tibia and fibula
together are severly damaged, the surgeon

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:28.450
will place a screw through both bones, or
a suture device may be placed through both

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:29.920
bones.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:34.680
Either of these devices will help hold the
bones together while the ligaments heal.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:41.040
At the end of the procedure, the surgeon will
close any skin incisions with stitches.

